    "result_text": "The build succeeds. The full suite ran green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephone numbers with minimal structural impact.\n\n**Changes:**\n1. **New file** `E164Telephone.java` \u2014 a small, cohesive utility class with a single static `isValid` method. It keeps the general 8\u201315 digit E.164 check and adds the country-specific rules: `+61` requires 9 national digits (11 total after `+`) and `+1` requires 10 national digits (11 total).\n\n2. **One-line edit** in `OwnerFieldsValidator.validate` \u2014 replaced the inline `e164.substring(1).matches(\"\\\\d{8,15}\")` check with a call to `E164Telephone.isValid(e164)`. Numbers failing the check are still rejected via the existing `errors.rejectValue(...)`, producing a 400.\n\n**Why this keeps structural cost low:** the new logic lives in its own tiny class (near-zero surrounding complexity / `WMC_other`), and the edit inside the existing validator is a single line to an unchanged control-flow, so I didn't grow any large method or inflate the `WMC_other` of a changed function.\n\nThe new acceptance test `errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (posting `+61 123`) now returns 400, and all previously-passing tests \u2014 including the valid `04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 `+61`+9-digit path \u2014 remain green.",
